Some afterthoughts:

Permanent is not the same as indestructible. My misconception was that I
interpreted "permanent" as only meaning "never degrades by only time
passing". I had of course read before that permanent was used to
describe engravings by wands of fire or lightning. Also I had noted in
games that engravings left to themselves did not change for tens of
thousands of turns. But these were engravings on which just one fight
had taken place. I used to think that if you fight long enough on such
an engraving you will eventually manage to damage it. That is at least
not illogical...

My approach to dealing with Demogorgon safely (using a boulder fort) was
devised for slash'em. I do not think that it would be a good *general*
approach in vanilla, even if you could determine on which level you meet
Demogorgon (to some extent you can: if he does not appear before Orcus's
level you should be able to make him appear there; the price to be paid
is that you lose all the initial charges from Orcus's wand). The point
is that in vanilla you do not have the same kind of missile weapons as
in slash'em. So you would have to rely on a bow, which is tedious if you
cannot get expert in it, or on spells and wands. This can take a long
time and exhaust some of your resources, so a melee approach not having
these disadvantages is much more attractive than in slash'em, where
there are firearms, which allow you to send five missiles per round even
at unskilled level. (There are very few roles that can become skilled in
firearms, but these skills are more or less wasted anyway, as firearms
are powerful enough without them.) Each of these bullets will do 1d30 +4
+2.5 damage, if enchanted to +4 (which is the level at which you will
hit all monsters) and blessed. So a Demogorgon at 200 Hp can be taken
down in 2 or 3 moves.

In vanilla, I would not use a scroll of earth at all. Just write
Elbereth on the downstairs of the level, where you are going to meet him
and drop some item on it. Then proceed as discussed. He will not be able
to occupy the stairs so when you come down, you will occupy it. There is
no need to create a boulder for this effect. If you are taking pets
along (on first descending, the second time you should not be
accompanied by a pet, because the pet might land on the staircase
instead of yourself), the object you drop should be cursed, so it will
not be removed by your pet. In slash'em, this strategy would of course
also work, but if you have some zombie pets, you should drop a blessed
item on the stairs, too. (Gugs like to pick up cursed items and will
move only reluctantly onto blessed ones.) This would be the most
resource-saving approach. What you spend is: two charges from a wand of
fire (for writing Elbereth twice), possibly one from a wand of digging
(to become visible to Demogorgon only after standing on an Elbereth
sqaure, which probably cannot be guaranteed in vanilla anyway), a scroll
of magic mapping (in slash'em) and either a cursed scroll of
teleportation (if you have teleport control) or a cursed potion of gain
level. Once you are on the stairs, you can happily whack at him with
your melee artifact and a silver saber (or two artifacts in slash'em) --
if you can twoweapon.

That I am reluctant to melee Demogorgon in slash'em may have to do with
him being much stronger than in vanilla -- at least that is my
impression. In my whole vanilla career, I met him only twice, and he
started out peacefully in both cases (not possible any longer?), so I
got to choose where to attack him. I did not it too difficult to get rid
of him. In slash'em, he is level 106 instead of 50, so I believe that he
also hits somewhat harder. In any case, I kept getting into trouble
meeting him in my early explore mode games, even died once while
backtracking to the stairs. Therefore, I developed a particular approach
for him. A bit of an overkill, maybe, but extremely safe.

